 ],
 "literal": "You indeed, not only the distress of the dove but also other / many afflictions, are the descendant of him who relieved! / These children indeed, fearing the distress of those who live by ploughing the field of learning and eating, / who dwell under the cool shade of sharing what they have, / having seen the elephant, crying, crying forgotten, / sparse-haired little children, looking bewildered at the public arena, / possess a new pain of distress. / If you have heard, you do what you desire!",
 "translation": "You are the descendant of the great king who relieved not only the distress of the dove but also many other afflictions! These children are the offspring of patrons under whose cool shade dwell scholars who plough the fields of learning, sharing whatever they have with others. Now, these sparse-haired little ones have even forgotten the crying they used to do when seeing an elephant; they just look bewildered at this assembly with a strange, new pain of distress. Now that you have heard this, do what you desire!",
 "modern": "புறாவின் துன்பத்தை மட்டும் அல்லாமல் வேறு பல துயரங்களையும் போக்கிய சிபிச் சக்கரவர்த்தியின் வழித்தோன்றல் நீ! இவர்களோ, கல்விப்புலத்தை உழுது வாழும் அறிஞர்கள் வறுமைக்கு அஞ்சித் தம்மிடம் உள்ளதைப் பகிர்ந்து உண்ணும் குளிர்ந்த நிழல் போன்ற கொடையாளியின் பிள்ளைகள். யானையைக் கண்டதும் அழும் அழுகையைக்கூட மறந்துவிட்டு, இந்தப் பொது அவையை மருட்சியோடு பார்த்து, புதியதொரு துன்ப நோயால் வாடுகிறார்கள் இந்தச் சிறுவர்கள். நான் கூறியதைக் கேட்டுவிட்டாய்; இனி உன் விருப்பப்படி செய்வாயாக!",
 "explanation": "The poet Kōvūr Kiḻār addresses the Cōḻa king Kiḷḷi Vaḷavaṉ to save the young sons of his enemy Malaiyamāṉ, whom the king has ordered to be trampled by an elephant. The poet begins by praising the king's lineage, reminding him that he is a descendant of Sibi, who famously sacrificed his own flesh to save a dove. He then contrasts this heritage of mercy with the innocent children, who are the offspring of generous patrons of scholars. The poet vividly describes the children's terror: they are so young that they usually cry at the sight of an elephant, but now, paralyzed by fear in the royal court, they have forgotten even to cry and look around in bewildered grief. By concluding with the powerful words, 'If you have heard this, do what you desire,' the poet successfully appeals to the king's honor and saves the children.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
